Allow both A/B slots for writable (allowlisted) partitions

partition_name_in_allowlist() pins the partition match to the slot
reported by androidboot.slot_suffix on the kernel cmdline and then
continues to the next allowlist entry, so the *inactive* slot is never
accepted. On OnePlus 6/6T (and any A/B device whose bootloader passes
slot_suffix) an untrusted process - e.g. KernelSU-su spawned kernel
flashers - can therefore write the current slot's boot/dtbo but gets
EPERM on the other slot, breaking inactive-slot kernel flashing and
manual OTA prep.

The allowlist entries are partitions meant to be writable (boot,
init_boot, vendor_boot, dtbo, vbmeta, userdata, ...; BLOCK_BOOT/
BLOCK_RECOVERY only remove boot/recovery from it). Accept both "_a"
and "_b" variants after the slot-suffix check fails instead of
skipping the entry.

Signed-off-by: OnePlus_SDM845_ReSukiSU_SUSFS builder
---
diff --git a/blkdev_helper.c b/blkdev_helper.c
index ffeacc6..5f2cd77 100644
--- a/blkdev_helper.c
+++ b/blkdev_helper.c
@@ -70,7 +70,14 @@ static bool partition_name_in_allowlist(const char *name, size_t max_len)
 						   allowed, slot_suffix))
 				return true;
 
-			continue;
+			/*
+			 * The allowlist covers partitions that are meant to be
+			 * writable (e.g. boot/dtbo when BLOCK_BOOT is off), so
+			 * both A/B slots must be accepted even when the kernel
+			 * cmdline pins the currently booted slot. Otherwise
+			 * writing the *inactive* slot - which OTA flows and
+			 * kernel flashers need - is wrongly denied.
+			 */
 		}
 
 		if (partition_name_matches(name, name_len,
